\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 a computer, a user, a sensor, or any other device capable of generating or capturing data.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 2. Receiver: The receiver is the destination of the transmitted data. It can
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0  be another computer, a display device, a storage device, or any other device that can r\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 eceive and process the data.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 3. Medium: The medium refers to the physical channel or pathway through which the data is transmitted. It can be wired, such as copper or fiber-optic cables, or wireless, such as radio waves or satellite links.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 4. Protocols: Pr\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
otocols are a set of rules and conventions that govern the formatting, transmission, and interpretation of data. They define how data is packaged, addressed, error-checked, and delivered between sender and receiver. Protocols ensure reliable and efficient
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0  \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 communication between devices. Examples include TCP/IP for internet communication or Ethernet for local area networks.
