s of flash disks, such as 2GB, 4GB, etc., typically follow a binary numbering system. In binary, each digit can have two possible values: 0 or 1. This binary system is used in digital electronics because it aligns with the underlying hardware architecture
,\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0  which is based on binary logic gates.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 The storage capacities of flash disks are determined by the number of binary digits, or bits, used to represent the data. In the case of storage sizes like 2GB, 4GB, 8GB, etc., the capacities are typically expressed i
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 n powers of two. This is because computers use binary addressing, and powers of two are convenient for representing memory sizes.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 When it comes to storage sizes, powers of two provide a natural way to increase capacity by adding more bits. For example, to \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
go from 2GB to 4GB, one additional bit is added to each storage unit. This pattern continues, allowing for easier scalability and efficient use of memory addressing in computer systems.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 On the manufacturing side, flash memory is typically produced in power\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
s of two capacities due to the way memory cells are organized and addressed. It simplifies the manufacturing process and makes it easier to ensure uniformity and compatibility across different devices.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 b. A 32-bit computer system can support a maximum of 4\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
GB of RAM due to its memory addressing limitations. In a 32-bit architecture, memory addresses are represented using 32 bits. Each bit can represent a binary value of 0 or 1, allowing for a total of 2^32 unique memory addresses.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 The memory address space is\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
 divided into different regions, including the RAM (Random Access Memory) used for temporary storage while the computer is running. With 32 bits, the addressable memory space is limited to 2^32 addresses, which is equivalent to 4GB (2^32 bytes).
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 The limita\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
tion arises because each memory address needs to be uniquely identified by a binary number. If the system were to support more than 4GB of RAM, it would require more memory addresses, which would exceed the capacity of a 32-bit architecture.
\par \hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 To overcome th\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 
is limitation, modern computer systems have transitioned to 64-bit architectures, which use 64-bit memory addresses. A 64-bit system can theoretically support up to 18.4 million terabytes of RAM, providing significantly more memory capacity compared to a 
\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 3\hich\af0\dbch\af31505\loch\f0 2-bit system.
